Route the real Node extension host around cp.fork() on iOS -- in-process,
via worker_threads, not a second OS-launched process.

iOS denies fork()/posix_spawn() to third-party processes outright, at the
sandbox-profile level -- unrelated to JIT or code signing, and with no known
workaround short of a full jailbreak. cp.fork() is exactly how this file
normally starts the extension host, so it can't run as-is.

Earlier revision of this patch: wrote the launch parameters
(bootstrapForkPath/args/env) to a JSON file in a shared App Group container,
picked up by a second NEPacketTunnelProvider extension (ExtensionHostRuntime)
that iOS itself launched as an independent OS process. That relied on
`com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension`, which Apple restricts to
paid Apple Developer Program accounts -- a free/Personal Team signing
identity gets denied at runtime (NEVPNErrorDomain Code=5,
"permission denied") no matter what the entitlements file says. See
ipad-vscode/README.md for the free-account architecture change this is
part of.

This revision instead runs bootstrap-fork.js in-process, in a
`worker_threads.Worker` on the SAME OS process as code-server's own server
(itself now kept alive in the background via a silent-audio AVAudioSession
loop in the main app -- see ipad-vscode/README.md -- rather than a
NetworkExtension process). The exthost protocol never needed a literal
parent/child relationship in the first place: on POSIX, when
_canSendSocket is false (forced here via IPADVSCODE_NO_FORK), the only
thing that happens is a plain net.Server listens on a Unix-domain-socket
path (createRandomIPCHandle()), and whatever process -- or, as it turns
out, whatever Worker thread within the same process -- connects to it is
treated as the extension host. bootstrap-fork.js and the real exthost
bootstrap chain it loads read their configuration (the named-pipe path
included) entirely from `env`/`argv`, not from anything that presumes a
real child_process IPC channel (`process.send` is used only for an
optional logging pipe-through, safely absent/no-op in a Worker), so no
patch to bootstrap-fork.js itself is needed -- only how
extensionHostConnection.ts launches it.

Correctness caveat, stated plainly because it hasn't been verified on a
real device: nodejs-mobile's embedded libnode build is not confirmed to
support worker_threads. It's core Node/V8 machinery (multiple Isolates
within one Platform), not an optional native addon, so there's good reason
to expect it works, but this has not been confirmed against an actual
build with no Mac/iPad available in this pipeline. If extension host
Workers fail to start, this is the first thing to check.

Known gap, unchanged from the previous revision: since there's no real
ChildProcess handle on this path, shortenReconnectionGraceTimeIfNecessary()
and the fd-passing branch of acceptReconnection() are inert, and the
exit/error lifecycle hooks fire from Worker events instead of ChildProcess
events. Not addressed here.

Only takes effect when IPADVSCODE_NO_FORK is set; behavior on every other
platform is unchanged.

Update (2026-09-01): the same ERR_WORKER_INVALID_EXEC_ARGV failure hit
again, for a different pair of flags, once real V8 JIT started running
on Simulator (see ipad-vscode's NodeRuntimeController.swift and
README.md's JIT section) -- "Initiated Worker with invalid execArgv
flags: --no-jitless, --no-expose-wasm", same "Failed to start
extension host process" symptom, confirmed via a real captured
node-stdio.log, not assumed. Extended the execArgv filter to also
strip --jitless/--no-jitless/--expose-wasm/--no-expose-wasm. Unlike
--max-old-space-size, these have no Worker-native resourceLimits
equivalent to translate to, and don't need one: jitless/expose_wasm
are process-wide V8 flags, already applied once via
V8::SetFlagsFromCommandLine() when this main process started, and
every Isolate the process creates afterwards -- including a Worker's
-- inherits that same global flag state automatically. Dropped, not
translated.

